/* Deep long-form expansions: Bhavishya, Brahmavaivarta, Varaha, Linga, Skanda. */
(() => {
  const D=window.SCRIPTURE_DETAIL_DATA=window.SCRIPTURE_DETAIL_DATA||{};
  const add=(name,sections,sources=[])=>{const key=`Purāṇa:${name}`,e=Object.assign({},D[name]||{},D[key]||{});D[key]=Object.assign({},e,{scholarlyDepth:'deep-v3',articleSections:[...(e.articleSections||[]),...sections],sources:[...(e.sources||[]),...sources]});};

  add('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Bhavishya Purana is not merely “late”; it is a textbook case of a Purana title remaining open to revision for an exceptionally long period. Its received printed corpus contains ritual and solar materials with medieval histories beside passages whose language, named communities and political references require much later dates. The title “of the future” made retrospective updating particularly easy because new history could be narrated as ancient prophecy.',
      'An old Bhavishya or Bhavishyat title certainly circulated. Early and medieval works know or quote a text by that name, yet many such citations cannot be located in the modern printed corpus. As with Brahma, continuity of title therefore does not guarantee continuity of textual body.',
      'The Brahmaparvan contains substantial ritual, solar, household and vrata materials that belong to the same medieval religious world as other Purana-dharmashastra compendia. These chapters should be dated through citations, parallels with Samba and other solar texts, calendrical institutions and the history of ritual digests rather than by association with the much later Pratisarga materials.',
      'The Madhyamaparvan contains developed ritual and Tantric-style materials and has its own chronology. Its relative technical coherence is one reason the four printed parvans should be treated almost as separate textual dossiers rather than one authorial sequence.',
      'The Pratisargaparvan is the most famous and most abused section. Parts of modern recensions refer to groups, rulers, religions and events belonging to early-modern and modern historical worlds. Such passages cannot be evidence that an ancient sage predicted Christianity, Islam, British rule or other later events. They are evidence that Purana authors continued to interpret new history through an inherited prophetic genre.',
      'The Uttaraparvan, with large bodies of vrata and festival material, again belongs to a different chronology. A historian must resist transferring the latest date of one Pratisarga passage to every Uttara vow, just as one must resist transferring the antiquity of an early ritual citation to the entire modern book.',
      'The Bhavishya therefore requires the most aggressively stratified dating in the corpus: identify parvan, chapter, recension and external witness before making historical claims. A sentence beginning “the Bhavishya Purana, composed in…” is almost always too simple.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'Modern printed editions commonly organize the corpus into four parvans: Brahma, Madhyama, Pratisarga and Uttara. A frequently cited arrangement gives 215 chapters to Brahmaparvan, three internal sections totalling about 62 chapters to Madhyama, four sections within Pratisarga, and about 208 chapters to Uttara. Manuscripts can differ sharply, and some self-descriptions mention five parts.',
      'The four parvans are not equal stages of one narrative. Brahma is largely a ritual-social and solar compendium; Madhyama contains specialized rites and technical religious material; Pratisarga uses recurring-creation or prophetic framing to narrate later historical worlds; Uttara is heavily devoted to vrata, festival and practical observance. The table of contents is therefore a redactional binding of distinct genres.',
      'Brahmaparvan itself moves through creation, samskara, brahmacharya, marriage, household norms, physical marks, Ganapati, serpent observances, varna-ashrama and a large solar ritual complex. The breadth makes it a source for medieval prescriptive religion far more substantial than popular fascination with “prophecies” suggests.',
      'Madhyamaparvan is structurally closer to a ritual manual. Its organization into sub-sections and its specialized procedures indicate incorporation of technical materials that should be compared with Tantra, paddhati and ritual digests.',
      'Pratisarga uses “renewed creation” as a literary licence to extend sacred history beyond classical dynastic horizons. This structural openness is the reason later material belongs there so naturally: a redactor can place contemporary or recent events inside an ancient speaker’s account of future ages.',
      'Uttara returns to the calendar. Large sequences of vows, fasts, sacred days and festivals make it one of the practically oriented portions of the text. The shift from future-history to recurring ritual time is conceptually significant: history changes, but the calendar repeatedly offers means of merit and purification.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Brahmaparvan — household, samskara and Sun worship',paragraphs:[
        'The opening parvan contains the most important material for correcting the popular image of the Bhavishya. It is full of initiation, conduct, marriage, domestic duty, bodily and calendrical observance, and extended Surya worship. The “future” is only one part of a much larger ritual book.',
        'Solar material includes names, images, festival days, vows and mythic narratives that should be compared with the Samba Purana and broader Saura literature. Similarity can reveal shared source traditions and medieval ritual networks.'
      ]},
      {title:'Madhyamaparvan — specialized ritual',paragraphs:[
        'Madhyama gathers ritual procedures whose technical detail is qualitatively different from broad Purana storytelling. Its relation to Tantric and paddhati literature is essential for dating and interpretation.',
        'The section demonstrates that Purana authority could absorb specialist ritual knowledge much as the Agni absorbed architecture and poetics. The sacred genre served as an encyclopedic host.'
      ]},
      {title:'Pratisargaparvan — prophecy as retrospective historiography',paragraphs:[
        'Pratisarga should be read as a history of historical imagination. Later authors can describe past events in future tense from the standpoint of an ancient narrator. The technique is not unique to this Purana, but the Bhavishya’s title makes it especially extensive.',
        'Foreign names, post-classical religions and recent political formations are therefore chronological clues. A passage containing them reveals the world of its redactor, often far more reliably than it reveals the world it claims to predict.',
        'This section must be controlled against manuscripts. Internet quotations frequently circulate without Sanskrit, chapter or edition, and some cannot be found in scholarly or even common printed witnesses.'
      ]},
      {title:'Uttaraparvan — ritual time and the expanding vrata archive',paragraphs:[
        'Uttara’s great value lies in the history of festivals and vows. Lunar dates, solar events, deity days and domestic observances are given myths and promised fruits. A later medieval household could thus enter sacred history through the calendar.',
        'Because vrata literature is intrinsically expandable, local or later observances can be inserted without breaking the genre. The very richness of Uttara is therefore a reason for chapter-level chronology.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'A full account of the Bhavishya must reverse the emphasis of popular summaries. Its major contribution is not a catalogue of miraculous predictions but a vast archive of ritualized time: samskaras, household duty, Surya worship, vratas, festivals and later attempts to bring changing political and religious history into a Purana cosmology.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Creation and religious education',paragraphs:[
        'Opening cosmological material establishes the Purana frame before the text moves quickly into human religious formation. Om, Gayatri, student discipline and purificatory rites place sacred speech and education at the beginning of the social life cycle.',
        'The sequence reflects a Brahmanical theory of person-making: one is not simply born into religious identity; samskara, learning and disciplined conduct repeatedly shape the person.'
      ]},
      {title:'Marriage, household and social norm',paragraphs:[
        'The Brahmaparvan devotes unusual attention to marriage, household conduct, relations between men and women and markers of auspiciousness. These chapters are normative and often highly conventional, but their detail makes them important for histories of medieval prescriptive domesticity.',
        'They should not be read as transparent ethnography. A rule can reveal what an author wanted to regulate precisely because actual practice was more varied.'
      ]},
      {title:'Ganapati, serpents and calendrical devotion',paragraphs:[
        'Ganapati and Naga observances show the calendar incorporating deities with distinct ritual functions: removal of obstacles, protection, fertility and household welfare. Purana narrative explains why a date or offering has power.',
        'Such chapters connect elite Sanskrit text to observances with wide popular afterlives. The historical relationship can move in both directions: compilers scripturalize existing practices, while scripture later reinforces them.'
      ]},
      {title:'The Saura corpus',paragraphs:[
        'Surya is one of the Bhavishya’s most important deities. Solar vows, iconography, names and festivals form a sustained dossier that belongs beside Samba and other Saura sources. This is central evidence for a religious tradition sometimes obscured by later Vaishnava-Shaiva dominance.',
        'The Sun’s relation to calendrical order makes the placement natural. Solar worship is simultaneously theology and timekeeping: days, transitions and seasons become ritually charged because the deity visibly structures time.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vrata as portable religion',paragraphs:[
        'The great vrata sequences convert sacred time into repeatable practice. Fasting, bathing, worship, lamp, story and gift can be performed in household settings without the resources required for royal sacrifice or distant pilgrimage.',
        'Each vow usually has an etiological story and a phalashruti. The story explains why the practice works; the promised result persuades repetition. The combination is one of the most successful literary forms in medieval Hindu religious culture.'
      ]},
      {title:'Future history and the problem of anachronism',paragraphs:[
        'Later Pratisarga passages name worlds recognizably later than classical Purana society. Rather than treating anachronism as embarrassment, a scholarly article asks what later redactors were doing: incorporating new religions, rulers and foreign peoples into a Sanskrit model of cyclical sacred history.',
        'The process can involve borrowing non-Indic narrative and terminology. That hybridity is evidence for contact and adaptation. It demonstrates that Purana literature remained open to the world around it rather than frozen after antiquity.',
        'Historical specificity is therefore the clue to value. A nineteenth-century passage is poor evidence for ancient prophecy but potentially excellent evidence for nineteenth-century Hindu attempts to narrate colonial and interreligious history.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'The Bhavishya has no uniform sectarian theology because its strata span different communities and centuries. Surya is exceptionally prominent in important older ritual blocks; Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva, Ganapati and Goddess traditions all appear elsewhere.',
      'Its most characteristic theological principle may be the sacralization of time. Calendar, future history and cosmic cycle are all ways of interpreting temporal change under divine order.',
      'Vrata theology emphasizes repeatable acts and promised fruits. The divine is approached through disciplined time rather than exclusively through philosophical knowledge or temple pilgrimage.',
      'Later historical sections can become polemical as new religious communities are fitted into Purana categories. Such passages reveal sectarian encounter, not an eternal theology of the whole book.',
      'The coexistence of very old ritual forms with modern additions demonstrates a theology of scriptural adaptability: sacred authority is preserved by continuously interpreting new conditions rather than by textual immobility.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Bhavishya’s ritual chapters influenced vrata and festival compendia far more deeply than modern sensational discussions suggest. Medieval and early-modern household religion could draw on it for calendars, fasts and solar observances.',
      'Saura scholars use the text as a major witness to Sun worship. Its relations with Samba materials reveal a network of solar myths, rituals and iconography that cannot be reconstructed from one Purana alone.',
      'The Pratisarga became disproportionately famous in modern print and internet culture because supposed predictions of later religions and colonial events attract apologetic interest. This reception often strips passages of manuscript and edition context.',
      'Nineteenth- and twentieth-century debates over “fraud” versus “interpolation” reflect differing expectations of scripture. Purana composition historically tolerated addition; modern readers shaped by fixed-book concepts can experience the same process as textual corruption.',
      'A better model is reception history: communities repeatedly wrote themselves into sacred time. Late additions are therefore historically meaningful even when their claims to ancient prediction cannot be accepted.',
      'The Bhavishya also shows how print can freeze one late recension and give it an appearance of canonical antiquity. Once widely distributed, a modern parvan can become the version most readers think is the text itself.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'The Brahma and Uttara parvans preserve a huge archive of prescriptive domestic religion: education, marriage, purity, festivals, fasts, gifts and worship. Their detail makes them valuable for history of norm, calendar and household ritual.',
      'Solar observances connect ritual to astronomy and seasonal life. A visible celestial cycle becomes the basis for repeated religious discipline.',
      'Gender and household rules are often highly prescriptive. They should be used to study ideals, anxieties and attempts at regulation, not to assume actual practice everywhere matched the text.',
      'Later historical passages are sources for social perception. Their categories for foreigners or other religions tell us how redactors understood difference in their own period, not how an ancient Hindu society classified future peoples.',
      'The long chronological span of the corpus makes internal dating indispensable for social history. A ritual chapter and a modern historical interpolation may be separated by many centuries even though they share one printed spine.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'No single complete critical edition resolves the Bhavishya’s enormous stratification. Modern printed editions can place very late material beside older chapters without signalling the historical distance between them.',
      'A responsible citation should give parvan, section, chapter and edition. For sensational historical claims, the Sanskrit witness and manuscript distribution are mandatory.',
      'External quotation is especially powerful. If an early digest quotes a Bhavishya verse, it establishes a terminus for that verse or a close ancestor. If a famous “prophecy” appears only in late prints, its historical status is correspondingly different.',
      'The corpus would benefit from separate critical editions of its ritual, solar and Pratisarga blocks rather than one attempt to force all strata into a single archetype.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Hazra and Rocher remain indispensable precisely because they treat the text source-critically rather than as a collection of fulfilled predictions. Studies of Saura religion should be used for Brahmaparvan, and modern-history passages require manuscript-focused work.',
      'Any online claim that “the Bhavishya predicted X” should be treated as a research question: locate the Sanskrit, identify recension and earliest witness, and only then discuss meaning.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'R. C. Hazra — Studies in the Puranic Records',detail:'Essential for ritual strata and chronology.'},{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard discussion of the highly layered Bhavishya tradition.'}]);

  add('Brahmavaivarta Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The surviving Brahmavaivarta Purana is a late medieval work even though the title itself is older. References to a Brahmavaivarta in earlier literature do not prove that those writers knew the four-khanda Krishna-Radha corpus now printed. The distinction between title history and extant recension is therefore fundamental.',
      'The received text’s developed theology of Krishna as supreme Brahman, Radha as his eternal power, Goloka as highest realm and the goddesses as forms of Prakriti belongs to a religious world in which medieval Krishna bhakti and Goddess theology had already developed substantially.',
      'Many scholars place the extant form late in the medieval period, with significant shaping around the fifteenth–sixteenth centuries, while recognizing that individual myths are older. The point is not to date every chapter to one century but to distinguish the final theological architecture from inherited narrative.',
      'Radha provides particularly strong relative evidence. She is marginal or absent as a named cosmic figure in earlier Sanskrit Krishna sources but becomes central here at every level of theology. The received Brahmavaivarta is therefore evidence for the historical elevation of Radha, not proof that all early Krishna tradition already possessed the same theology.',
      'The four khandas themselves may contain layers. Prakriti collects Goddess traditions of different origin; Ganesha narratives adapt a deity with independent Purana histories; Krishna-janma draws on older Krishna cycles but places them in a new Goloka-Radha frame.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The common recension has four khandas: Brahma, Prakriti, Ganesha and Krishna-janma. Common chapter counts are about 30, 67, 46 and 131–133 respectively, for a total around 274–276. The extraordinary length of Krishna-janma shows where the received redaction places its narrative centre.',
      'Brahma Khanda establishes Krishna as primordial source and reframes creation around him. Prakriti Khanda organizes major goddesses and feminine powers through a unified Prakriti theology. Ganesha Khanda absorbs the elephant-headed god into Krishna-centred cosmic hierarchy. Krishna-janma then elaborates Radha, Goloka and the earthly Krishna narrative at much greater length.',
      'The structure is therefore hierarchical rather than merely topical. Each khanda progressively demonstrates how a different sphere—creation, Goddess power, Ganesha and Krishna narrative—belongs to one Krishna-Radha metaphysics.',
      'The traditional eighteen-thousand-verse claim interacts unusually with manuscripts that can exceed that number, unlike Puranas whose extant texts fall far short of traditional totals. This is another reminder that canonical verse numbers are not reliable measures of an original archetype.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Brahma Khanda — Krishna as Brahman and source',paragraphs:[
        'The opening khanda radicalizes the title “transformation of Brahman” by identifying supreme Brahman with Krishna. Other gods and cosmic principles emerge from him rather than merely cooperating as independent creators.',
        'Radha appears very early as inseparable divine power, which means the later enormous Krishna section is already encoded into the cosmology. Creation itself is interpreted through divine couplehood.'
      ]},
      {title:'Prakriti Khanda — many goddesses, one feminine power',paragraphs:[
        'Lakshmi, Sarasvati, Savitri, Durga, Ganga, Tulasi, Shashthi and other goddesses receive myths and worship, yet the redaction interprets them through a theory of primordial Prakriti and ultimately Radha.',
        'This is not identical to Devi Bhagavata Shaktism. Feminine divinity is enormously exalted, but the hierarchy remains Krishna-Radha centred. Comparison reveals how similar Goddess categories can be used inside different sectarian systems.'
      ]},
      {title:'Ganesha Khanda — incorporating a popular independent deity',paragraphs:[
        'Ganesha’s birth and exploits are retold inside the Krishna-centred framework. The Purana can treat him as a manifestation or derivative of the highest reality without denying his distinctive ritual role.',
        'The khanda illustrates sectarian incorporation: a deity with major independent traditions is not excluded but genealogically and metaphysically relocated.'
      ]},
      {title:'Krishna-janma Khanda — Radha, Goloka and divine play',paragraphs:[
        'The final khanda is far more than a “birth” chapter. It builds an entire Krishna-Radha sacred universe: eternal Goloka, descent into earthly narrative, Vraja play, demons, Kamsa, later royal life and the eventual Yadava end.',
        'The key transformation is ontological. Vraja is not merely childhood geography but an earthly manifestation of eternal Goloka, and Radha is not only a beloved cowherd woman but the eternal power inseparable from supreme Krishna.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The received Brahmavaivarta should be read as a late medieval systematic reorganization of familiar Hindu divine figures around Krishna and Radha. Creation, goddesses, Ganesha and Krishna mythology are not separate encyclopedic topics; each demonstrates how the whole pantheon and cosmos can be derived from a supreme erotic-devotional pair.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Cosmogony from Krishna',paragraphs:[
        'The Purana’s creation model begins not from an impersonal material sequence alone but from Krishna as supreme reality. Narayana, Shiva, Brahma and other divine powers arise within his manifestation.',
        'This transforms older Purana hierarchy. Krishna no longer enters late as an avatara inside a cosmos already made by Brahma; the cosmos and its gods appear within a Krishna-centred metaphysics.'
      ]},
      {title:'Radha before the earthly story',paragraphs:[
        'Radha’s theological significance is established before the long Vraja narrative. She belongs to the eternal structure of divine reality rather than emerging merely as one human lover in Krishna’s biography.',
        'This allows later romantic narratives to bear metaphysical weight. Separation, jealousy and reunion can be interpreted as divine play between eternal principles rather than ordinary social episodes.'
      ]},
      {title:'Goddess myths as Prakriti theology',paragraphs:[
        'The Prakriti Khanda collects myths that elsewhere circulate in Shakta, Vaishnava and local contexts. By interpreting goddesses as expressions of one primordial feminine reality, the redactor creates a theological taxonomy from a plural ritual landscape.',
        'Tulasi, Ganga and other figures connect cosmic femininity to household and pilgrimage practice. Plant, river and goddess become different modes of sacred power.'
      ]},
      {title:'Ganesha and sectarian synthesis',paragraphs:[
        'Ganesha’s inclusion shows the practical need for a comprehensive Purana theology. A household and temple deity too important to ignore is given a place in the Krishna universe rather than left as a competing centre.',
        'His stories also preserve independent ritual motifs—obstacle removal, beginnings, family relations—that are not dissolved by the metaphysical hierarchy.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vraja as eternal geography',paragraphs:[
        'The Krishna-janma Khanda elevates Vraja by making earthly play a manifestation of Goloka. Pastoral landscape becomes theology: cows, groves, gopis and play are not a temporary childhood stage to be outgrown when Krishna becomes prince.',
        'This differs sharply from the structural logic of the Vishnu Purana, where Vraja is a compact portion of an avatara biography. The Brahmavaivarta’s late medieval theology makes pastoral intimacy ontologically ultimate.'
      ]},
      {title:'Radha-Krishna eros and theological risk',paragraphs:[
        'Erotic narrative becomes a language for absolute intimacy, but it also creates interpretive problems around social norms, sexuality and divine conduct. Later devotional communities develop sophisticated distinctions between mundane desire and divine rasa to protect the theological reading.',
        'A historical article should not sanitize these passages into abstract symbolism or reduce them to scandal. Their importance lies precisely in the attempt to make human erotic vocabulary bear a claim about the highest reality.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'Krishna is supreme Brahman and source rather than merely an avatara of Vishnu. Narayana and other gods can be manifestations within Krishna’s ultimate identity. This is one of the received Purana’s most distinctive doctrinal reversals.',
      'Radha is inseparable divine power. Her relationship to Krishna can be described through Prakriti, Shakti and eternal beloved, allowing feminine divinity to occupy a position far more central than in earlier mainstream Krishna Puranas.',
      'Goloka stands above ordinary cosmic realms as the eternal setting of Krishna-Radha play. This means liberation can be imagined not only as escape from samsara or residence in Vaikuntha but participation in an eternal pastoral-divine world.',
      'The Prakriti theology assimilates many goddesses without becoming identical to Shaktism. Their unity is interpreted inside the supremacy of Krishna-Radha, demonstrating the difference between divine-feminine exaltation and Goddess-centred canonical supremacy.',
      'Ganesha, Shiva and other deities are included through emanation and relationship. The theology is comprehensive because it can absorb popular cults without relinquishing its centre.',
      'Bhakti is intensely personal and affective. The cosmic source is approached not only by metaphysical knowledge but through the emotional grammar of love, longing and play.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Brahmavaivarta became especially important for later Sanskrit articulations of Radha’s cosmic status. It is frequently cited in modern discussions seeking Purana authority for Radha-Krishna theology.',
      'Its late date makes that use historically revealing rather than invalid: the text witnesses a stage when Radha had become central enough to reorganize creation itself. It should not be used to erase the developmental gap between early Krishna sources and late medieval theology.',
      'Bengal and North Indian devotional environments gave the text particular resonance, though its relation to specific later Vaishnava schools is not reducible to direct dependence. Shared concepts can reflect a broader devotional milieu.',
      'Prakriti Khanda stories circulate independently in vrata, Goddess and household traditions. Many readers encounter Tulasi, Ganga or Shashthi narratives without knowing their place in the four-khanda architecture.',
      'Ganesha material likewise participates in a wider Purana network. Comparison with Shiva and Ganesha-focused traditions shows how sectarian texts continuously appropriate common divine biographies.',
      'Modern apologetic reception often cites the Brahmavaivarta as if it were as old as the earliest Purana layers. A scholarly presentation instead makes its late medieval creativity the reason it matters.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'The Purana combines high Radha-Krishna theology with conventional domestic, purity and social prescriptions. Divine erotic transcendence therefore coexists with a normative household world that can be conservative.',
      'Goddess and plant/river worship bring theology into everyday practice. Tulasi, Ganga and other sacred feminine forms connect household space and pilgrimage to the cosmic Prakriti scheme.',
      'Krishna devotion includes recitation, name, festival and story as accessible practices. These forms allow the late medieval theology to circulate beyond specialized philosophical audiences.',
      'The social meaning of Radha should not be inferred directly from social rules for historical women. A text can place a feminine divine figure at the metaphysical summit without dismantling ordinary gender hierarchy.',
      'Stories of curse, birth and caste mixture in some sections are prescriptive mythologies of social difference. They should be studied as attempts to explain hierarchy rather than accepted as historical ethnography.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The received four-khanda Brahmavaivarta lacks a universally adopted critical edition capable of reconstructing the relation between modern manuscripts and earlier references to the title.',
      'The principal textual problem is historical identity: how much, if any, of an older Brahmavaivarta survives inside the late four-khanda work? Answering requires external quotations and manuscript dating rather than doctrinal speculation.',
      'Chapter counts differ slightly, especially in Krishna-janma. Citations should therefore name edition.',
      'Comparative editing of Radha and Prakriti passages against other late medieval Krishna and Goddess texts would help identify shared sources and redactional innovation.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher remains the safest overview for distinguishing the old title from the received late work. Histories of Radha-Krishna devotion should be used to place its theology in medieval context.',
      'Comparison with Bhagavata and Devi Bhagavata is especially productive because all three can claim Purana authority while constructing very different relations among Krishna, feminine divinity and ultimate reality.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Textual history of the Brahmavaivarta title and received recension.'}]);

  add('Varāha Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Varaha Purana is another case in which the deity named in the title does not predict the contents of the extant book. The received corpus is heavily shaped by pilgrimage, vrata and regional sacred geography, and traditional verse totals far exceed the surviving text. This suggests substantial loss, replacement or recensional transformation.',
      'Major received materials belong to the medieval period, while many myths and Vaishnava theological motifs are older. Chapter-level dating should use sacred-place history, citation in ritual digests, relation to other mahatmyas and manuscript distribution.',
      'The Mathura and other pilgrimage materials are especially useful for historical geography. A mahatmya may presuppose an established network of temples and tirthas without being contemporary with the earliest sanctity of those places.',
      'Goddess and Shaiva materials likewise show compilation across sectarian boundaries. Their presence should not be explained as accidental corruption of an original pure Varaha text; Purana growth routinely incorporated regional cults.',
      'The gap between traditional extent and surviving chapters cautions against assuming that the modern Varaha preserves the same textual body summarized in every medieval Purana list.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The received Varaha is transmitted as a long chapter sequence rather than a universally stable multi-skandha architecture. Its strongest internal units are thematic: Varaha-Vishnu dialogue, creation and dharma, Goddess traditions, vrata and large pilgrimage mahatmyas.',
      'Dialogue framing allows these units to be joined with little narrative transition. The boar avatara can serve as authoritative speaker even when the actual subject is a local tirtha or Goddess rite.',
      'The lack of a strong book division makes internal mapping more important. A reader should identify when the text has shifted from cosmological teaching to mahatmya, from mahatmya to vrata or from Vaishnava myth to a local Goddess dossier.',
      'Traditional catalogues describe a larger Varaha than survives. The extant architecture may therefore be the residue of a significantly changed Purana rather than a complete short recension of an originally short book.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Varaha frame and Vaishnava teaching',paragraphs:[
        'Varaha provides the divine authority for instruction. The earth-rescuing avatara naturally links cosmology to geography: the god who raises the earth can explain the sacred places distributed across it.',
        'This frame is particularly suitable for pilgrimage expansion because local landscapes can be interpreted as parts of the earth restored and sanctified by Vishnu.'
      ]},
      {title:'Mahatmya blocks',paragraphs:[
        'Large sacred-geography units behave as independent works inside the Purana. They have their own place names, myths, ritual promises and itineraries.',
        'Their internal coherence makes it possible for them to circulate, expand or be replaced more readily than tightly integrated narrative chapters.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Varaha Purana is best understood as a Vaishnava-authorized map of sacred practice rather than a biography of the boar avatara. Its contents move through myth, pilgrimage, Goddess worship, vows and dharma with geography as a major organizing force.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'The earth and sacred geography',paragraphs:[
        'Varaha’s relation to the earth gives the Purana a natural theological basis for describing regions. The physical earth is not merely matter rescued from waters; it becomes a network of places where divine action remains accessible.',
        'Mahatmya literature then specifies how to activate that sacred geography: bath, sight, fast, gift and narrative remembrance turn a location into a tirtha.'
      ]},
      {title:'Mathura and Vaishnava landscape',paragraphs:[
        'Mathura material participates in the Sanskrit construction of Krishna’s sacred geography. The city and surrounding sites are connected to mythic events and ritual rewards, helping transform narrative memory into pilgrimage routes.',
        'Comparison with Bhagavata, Harivamsha and later Braj mahatmyas reveals how Krishna geography grew over time. The Varaha is one witness within that developing network.'
      ]},
      {title:'Goddess materials',paragraphs:[
        'The received Purana includes important Goddess traditions that complicate a purely Vaishnava label. Local female deities and major Goddess forms can be incorporated into the earth-centred sacred map.',
        'This is consistent with the mahatmya method: regional religious reality is included rather than erased, while the Varaha frame supplies overarching authority.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vrata and the ritual calendar',paragraphs:[
        'Vows create sacred time in parallel to pilgrimage’s sacred space. Fast, worship, gift and story can reproduce the merit of major religious acts in recurring household settings.',
        'Because vrata units are easily expandable, their chronology may differ sharply from neighbouring mythic chapters.'
      ]},
      {title:'Dharma and liberation',paragraphs:[
        'Prescriptive chapters connect pilgrimage and vow to broader conduct, gift, purity and social order. Religious merit is accumulated through a network of practices rather than one exclusive path.',
        'Vaishnava devotion can ultimately redirect such merit toward liberation, but the text still preserves strong interest in worldly and post-mortem fruits.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'Vishnu and Varaha provide the nominal theological frame, especially through the relationship between divine rescue and sacred earth. Yet the received text is religiously plural in its local materials.',
      'Sacred geography is itself theological: divine presence is distributed across the earth in places whose stories make cosmic events locally accessible.',
      'The Goddess can occupy important regional and ritual roles without displacing the Vaishnava frame. Hierarchical inclusion is more accurate than a claim of doctrinal uniformity.',
      'Vrata and pilgrimage emphasize divine grace mediated through time and space. Liberation theology coexists with promised worldly and ancestral benefits.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Varaha’s main influence lies in pilgrimage traditions. Regional mahatmyas could authorize Mathura and other sacred landscapes by placing them under Mahapurana revelation.',
      'Its Krishna geography participates in the broader textual making of Braj. Later pilgrimage traditions intensify and localize many themes already visible in Purana literature.',
      'Goddess materials show the text functioning as a container for local cults. A regional deity can gain pan-Indian scriptural context without losing local specificity.',
      'Modern summaries often overstate Varaha narrative because of the title. A deeper reading corrects reception by showing that sacred geography and ritual are the real bulk of the extant work.',
      'The discrepancy between traditional verse total and surviving text has also shaped scholarship, encouraging models of loss and replacement rather than simple late interpolation.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'Pilgrimage dominates practical religion: bathing, seeing, circumambulating, giving and hearing at sacred places. The mahatmyas imply networks of pilgrims and ritual specialists while remaining normative literary constructions.',
      'Vratas organize household time, making merit accessible through recurring observance. Their stories often connect family welfare, fertility or prosperity to devotion.',
      'Dharma passages preserve Brahmanical social ideals whose historical application must be tested independently.',
      'Regional sacred geography can be compared with archaeology and inscription to distinguish literary imagination from institutional history.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The Varaha lacks a universally accepted complete critical edition. The large gap between traditional extent and extant recensions makes manuscript cataloguing especially important.',
      'Mahatmya units should be edited independently because they may circulate outside the host Purana or differ regionally.',
      'Translations based on one printed sequence can conceal recension difference. Edition and chapter numbering should accompany citations.',
      'A future textual history must distinguish loss of an older Varaha from later accretion to the surviving corpus; both processes may have occurred.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher and Hazra provide the broad textual and chronological framework. Detailed study should follow the sacred region or ritual unit under investigation.',
      'For Mathura/Braj, Purana evidence must be combined with specialist historical-geography scholarship rather than treated as a self-dating record.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Survey of Varaha Purana textual history.'},{title:'R. C. Hazra — Studies in the Puranic Records',detail:'Useful for ritual chronology.'}]);

  add('Liṅga Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Linga Purana’s oldest core has been dated broadly between the mid-first millennium and early medieval centuries, but the received two-part work is demonstrably layered. Its own conclusion states a chapter count for the Uttara-bhaga smaller than that found in common manuscripts, direct internal evidence of expansion.',
      'Purva-bhaga’s 108 chapters contain the main Shaiva cosmology, linga theology, myths and ritual traditions. Uttara-bhaga commonly has 55 chapters, while an internal verse refers to 46; the extra chapters or a larger redaction therefore postdate the self-description preserved by the text.',
      'The appearance of Pashupata yoga, Nakulisha traditions, temple and linga ritual allows comparison with historical Shaiva institutions. Such evidence can date particular doctrinal environments without proving that all 163 chapters were composed together.',
      'The linga myth itself is older than the extant Purana. Fiery-pillar and aniconic Shiva traditions occur elsewhere, so the date of a theological motif must be distinguished from the date of this redaction.',
      'The received work’s broad inclusion of Vishnu, Brahma, Vedic material, cosmology and astronomy again argues against imagining one author writing a single sectarian manifesto.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The common two-part architecture is unusually clear: Purva-bhaga has 108 chapters; Uttara-bhaga has 55 in many editions. The discrepancy with the internal statement of 46 Uttara chapters makes the closing section a built-in witness to textual growth.',
      'Purva develops the theology of linga, creation, geography, dynasties, Shiva manifestations, myths, rites and cosmology. Uttara continues linga praise and ritual, adds vows, gifts, geography, ethical instruction and culminates in yogic teaching.',
      'The structure moves between nishkala and sakala Shiva: formless or attributeless supremacy is represented through the linga, while narrative chapters give Shiva embodied forms, deeds, family and avatara-like manifestations. Ritual and myth are two modes of making the same deity accessible.',
      'The final yoga chapters make internal realization the culmination of external linga worship. This does not invalidate temple ritual; the text creates a graded path from installed sign and mantra toward consciousness of Shiva beyond form.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Purva-bhaga — cosmic linga and Shaiva world history',paragraphs:[
        'The first part establishes Shiva’s supremacy through linga theology and then expands into a complete Purana world. Creation, cosmic geography, dynasties and demon conflicts are narrated in ways that place Shiva at the ontological centre.',
        'This is important methodologically: the linga is not one ritual topic among many. It is the interpretive principle by which an entire Purana cosmos is rendered Shaiva.'
      ]},
      {title:'Uttara-bhaga — expansion, ritual and yoga',paragraphs:[
        'The second part intensifies worship, vrata, gift and mantra before turning to liberation. Its variable extent suggests later addition to an older core, perhaps through stages rather than one insertion.',
        'The conclusion’s remembered 46-chapter total provides rare internal evidence that the book knew an earlier shorter Uttara.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Linga Purana answers two connected questions: why Shiva is worshipped through the linga, and how that linga-centred theology can organize the same cosmology, social practice and liberation teaching expected from a Mahapurana.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Lingodbhava — the immeasurable pillar',paragraphs:[
        'The fiery column encountered by Brahma and Vishnu dramatizes transcendence through failed measurement. Neither god can find beginning or end. Shiva’s sign is therefore not a miniature body but an icon of infinitude.',
        'The story has sectarian force because it subordinates Brahma and Vishnu, but it also provides philosophical rationale for aniconic worship. The linga can represent the unbounded precisely by refusing a bounded anthropomorphic outline.'
      ]},
      {title:'Nishkala and sakala',paragraphs:[
        'The text distinguishes Shiva beyond attributes from Shiva manifest in forms. This prevents formlessness and image worship from becoming contradictions. The linga mediates the transition, while myths and icons express qualified manifestations.',
        'Ritual theory follows: installation and worship can address a form without claiming the supreme is exhausted by that form.'
      ]},
      {title:'Creation and cosmography',paragraphs:[
        'Shaiva supremacy is extended into cosmogony. Elements, worlds, continents, planets and beings unfold within a Shiva-centred cosmic order while retaining categories shared with other Puranas.',
        'The reuse of common cosmology is itself sectarian technique: one need not invent a different universe to make it Shaiva; one reinterprets the source and hierarchy of the familiar universe.'
      ]},
      {title:'Shiva manifestations and Nakulisha',paragraphs:[
        'The Purana narrates multiple Shiva forms and includes traditions associated with Nakulisha/Lakulisha, the teacher linked to Pashupata history. Such passages are especially valuable because they connect mythic Purana narration with identifiable Shaiva institutional traditions.',
        'They must nevertheless be compared with inscriptions and independent Pashupata sources before being converted into linear history.'
      ]},
      {title:'Linga installation and ritual',paragraphs:[
        'Consecration, material, proportion, bathing, mantra and daily worship translate cosmic linga theology into temple practice. The ritual object becomes a localized axis through which the infinite is approached.',
        'Myths of successful worship illustrate efficacy. Narrative persuades where technical instruction alone might not: kings, sages and devotees demonstrate what linga devotion can accomplish.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vows, gifts and sacred geography',paragraphs:[
        'Vrata and dāna chapters connect Shiva devotion to household time and social exchange. Tirtha material maps divine presence across rivers and places.',
        'These practices expand Shaivism beyond specialized initiates. One can enter the Purana’s religious world through pilgrimage, gift or fasting as well as through esoteric knowledge.'
      ]},
      {title:'Pashupata yoga and release',paragraphs:[
        'The final yoga teaching moves from mantra and breath toward subtler forms of contemplation. The goal is absorption or union with supreme Shiva beyond ordinary identity.',
        'The progression from external sign to internal realization gives the entire Purana a soteriological direction. Linga worship is not left as an end in material ritual; it can become a doorway to knowledge of the unbounded reality the linga symbolizes.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'Shiva is supreme in both nishkala and sakala modes. The Purana’s distinctive achievement is to make the linga the conceptual bridge between those modes.',
      'Brahma and Vishnu remain powerful but are subordinated in lingodbhava and other narratives. Their inability to measure Shiva dramatizes a hierarchy that can still allow them honour within the cosmic order.',
      'Shakti and divine manifestation are necessary for action. The Purana’s Shaivism is not simply an abstract cult of formlessness; Goddess, family and embodied myths make the supreme active in the world.',
      'Pashupata and yogic themes bring institutional Shaivism into the Purana. Bondage and liberation can be interpreted through the relation of Lord, soul and limiting conditions.',
      'Ritual and knowledge form a continuum. The linga can be installed and bathed, meditated upon and finally understood as a sign of the reality beyond every finite object.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Linga Purana became a major narrative and ritual authority for linga worship. Its infinite-pillar myth circulates widely in temple legend, festival and visual art.',
      'Nakulisha/Pashupata materials make it important for histories of Shaiva institutions, although their Purana redaction must be compared with earlier evidence.',
      'Temple and vrata chapters contributed to the wider Sanskrit ritual culture of Shiva worship. Their influence is often difficult to distinguish from parallel Agamic traditions because the same practices circulate across genres.',
      'The two-part textual history itself influenced modern scholarship by providing unusually explicit evidence of expansion through the 46-versus-55 chapter discrepancy.',
      'Modern popular interpretation often explains the linga narrowly as a phallic symbol or, conversely, denies all generative symbolism. The Purana’s own theology is broader: axis, sign, infinity, creation and divine presence overlap. Historical interpretation should preserve that semantic range.',
      'The text’s inclusion of Vishnu and Vedic themes also complicates modern sectarian boundaries. Shaiva supremacy does not require literary isolation from broader Brahmanical culture.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'Linga installation and worship presuppose temples, patrons, ritual specialists and material production. These chapters can be compared with inscriptions and archaeology to study the institutionalization of Shaiva worship.',
      'Vratas make Shiva devotion portable into household calendars, while pilgrimage distributes it across sacred geography. The Purana therefore addresses more than monastic or initiated communities.',
      'Dāna and social duties preserve conventional Brahmanical norms. Shaiva theology operates inside a wider social order rather than replacing it.',
      'Pashupata yoga introduces ascetic discipline and a religious identity that can stand in tension with household norm. The Purana’s breadth allows both paths to coexist.',
      'The material history of the linga—stone, installation, temple maintenance—also connects theology to artisans and economic patronage even when the text foregrounds priests and kings.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The Linga Purana survives in multiple versions and lacks a universally definitive complete critical edition. The 108/55 structure is a common received form, not proof of original chapter boundaries.',
      'The internal assertion of 46 Uttara chapters should be treated as primary textual evidence for expansion. A critical history must identify which nine or more chapters belong to later stages and whether growth occurred once or repeatedly.',
      'Pashupata-related passages deserve independent collation because of their historical importance. Sectarian interest can encourage later normalization.',
      'Translations are useful for survey but must identify their Sanskrit base. Chapter offsets between editions can otherwise create false parallelism.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher is the essential structural overview. Histories of Pashupata Shaivism and Lakulisha should be used for the institutional passages, while Agamic and temple scholarship is needed for ritual chapters.',
      'The best reading method is to move repeatedly between the Purana’s linga theology and the material/religious history of actual linga worship rather than reducing either to the other.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard Linga Purana survey.'},{title:'J. L. Shastri, trans. — The Linga Purana',detail:'Complete English access to a major received recension.'}]);

  add('Skanda Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'No Mahapurana requires a sharper distinction between an early text and later corpora sharing its title. The early Skandapurana reconstructed by the Groningen project from old Nepalese manuscripts is a relatively compact Shaiva work whose core belongs to the early medieval period. The enormous printed “Skanda Purana” of later north Indian editions is a library of khandas and mahatmyas assembled over many centuries.',
      'Old palm-leaf manuscripts preserve a text markedly different from the gigantic seven-khanda vulgate. Adriaensen, Bakker and Isaacson’s critical-edition project demonstrated that this early Skandapurana is not simply the first few chapters of the later encyclopedia. It represents an earlier Shaiva Purana tradition with its own structure and historical geography.',
      'The later khandas—Kashi, Avanti, Nagara, Prabhasa and others depending on recension—contain extensive regional pilgrimage literature. Their dates must be argued independently through named temples, regional dynasties, sectarian institutions, citations and manuscript age.',
      'The Reva Khanda exemplifies title migration. Modern print history assigned a long Narmada text to Skanda, while manuscript scholarship distinguishes different Reva traditions and associates another long recension with Vayu. “Skanda Purana says” is therefore meaningless without specifying which Skanda corpus.',
      'The title’s durability is historically remarkable. An early Shaiva scripture could become the canonical umbrella under which medieval and early-modern sacred geographies from across India accumulated. Continuity of name enabled radical expansion of content.',
      'Dating the Skanda Purana consequently begins by naming the object: early critically edited Skandapurana; a specific later khanda; a regional mahatmya; or a modern vulgate assembly. Only after that identification is a date meaningful.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The early Skandapurana and the later multi-khanda corpus must not be forced into one table of contents. The early text is edited adhyaya by adhyaya from Nepalese manuscript evidence and centres on Shaiva sacred history, Pashupata-related themes, geography and myth. The later vulgate is an enormous anthology of regional books.',
      'Common later arrangements speak of seven major khandas, but their names and contents vary by recension and printing. Maheshvara, Vaishnava, Brahma, Kashi, Avanti, Nagara and Prabhasa are familiar large divisions in one tradition, while other editions redistribute or supplement material.',
      'Each khanda can itself contain independent mahatmyas. Kashi can function as a city scripture; Avanti maps Ujjain and surrounding sites; Prabhasa organizes the western pilgrimage landscape; Reva maps the Narmada. The whole is therefore a federation of sacred geographies rather than a single continuous narrative.',
      'The early text is important precisely because it reveals what came before that federation. Its manuscript coherence allows scholars to trace the growth of Shaiva mythology and institutions without assuming the later pilgrimage encyclopedia already existed.',
      'Speaker frames and Purana authority made later incorporation possible. A regional text could be narrated by an appropriate sage, linked to Skanda or Shiva and thereby become part of a Mahapurana title even if its original circulation was local.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'The early Skandapurana',paragraphs:[
        'The Groningen critical edition begins from the oldest recoverable manuscript tradition rather than the nineteenth-century vulgate. Its chapters develop Shiva’s supremacy, sacred places, mythic history and religious communities within a comparatively coherent early Shaiva framework.',
        'This early corpus is a primary source for the history of Shaivism because it stands closer to the period in which Pashupata and related institutions were expanding. Its geographical references can sometimes be compared with archaeology and inscriptions to locate religious networks.'
      ]},
      {title:'Kashi Khanda — the city as cosmic centre',paragraphs:[
        'The later Kashi material turns Varanasi into a complete salvific geography. Ghats, lingas, wells, shrines and routes are connected by myths explaining why death, bathing, worship and residence there possess exceptional force.',
        'The city is not merely one tirtha among others. Shaiva theology makes Kashi a place where ordinary rules of death are transformed by Shiva’s presence. The mahatmya thus converts urban topography into a map of liberation.'
      ]},
      {title:'Avanti and regional Shaiva geography',paragraphs:[
        'Avanti material centres Ujjain and surrounding tirthas. Mahakala and other local forms are integrated into the pan-Indian Shiva world through narrative, ritual and pilgrimage circuits.',
        'Such texts are vital for regional history because they preserve how communities imagined connections among temples. They should be compared with inscriptions and archaeology rather than treated as simple documentary gazetteers.'
      ]},
      {title:'Prabhasa and western sacred landscape',paragraphs:[
        'Prabhasa material organizes a western Indian pilgrimage field associated with Somnath and a wider network of tirthas. Mythic events, especially those linked to Shiva and epic-Purana memory, authorize movement through the region.',
        'The history of the physical Somnath temple and the literary history of Prabhasa must be distinguished; a mahatmya can preserve older sacred identity while incorporating later institutional change.'
      ]},
      {title:'Reva/Narmada traditions',paragraphs:[
        'Narmada pilgrimage literature demonstrates the mobility of mahatmya corpora. Different Reva Khandas can be assigned to different Purana titles in manuscripts and print.',
        'The river is sacralized through a continuous chain of tirthas, origin stories and promised merit. Parikrama traditions give this literature a living spatial counterpart, but the historical relation between text and practice must be established locally.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Skanda tradition is less a book than a history of Shaiva expansion across sacred space. The early Purana builds a mythic and institutional Shaiva world; later khandas repeatedly localize that world in cities, rivers, mountains and regional temple networks.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Shiva, Skanda and the early Shaiva narrative field',paragraphs:[
        'Although named for Skanda, the early text is fundamentally a Shaiva Purana in which Shiva’s cosmic and sacred presence organizes the narrative. Skanda’s role belongs within a larger mythology of divine family, demons, sacred geography and religious transmission.',
        'The title may reflect narrative authority and sectarian lineage more than the proportion of chapters devoted to Skanda’s biography.'
      ]},
      {title:'Pashupata worlds',paragraphs:[
        'The early Skandapurana is especially important for the history of Pashupata-related Shaivism. Ascetics, sacred sites and theological categories appear in a period close enough to institutional evidence to permit historical comparison.',
        'Purana narrative can thereby preserve religious geography that more abstract philosophical texts omit: where practitioners locate divine events matters as much as what they teach.'
      ]},
      {title:'Tirtha-mahatmya as an expanding genre',paragraphs:[
        'Later Skanda growth is driven by mahatmya. A place receives mythic origin, ritual prescriptions, lists of subsidiary sites and phalashruti. The form is modular and can expand indefinitely.',
        'This explains the enormous size of later vulgates without requiring one editor to plan tens of thousands of verses. Regional books accumulated under a title prestigious enough to universalize local sacred worlds.'
      ]},
      {title:'Kashi — death, Taraka and liberation',paragraphs:[
        'Kashi theology often centres the claim that Shiva grants liberating knowledge or mantra at death. The ordinary terror of dying becomes a special privilege of place.',
        'Ghats, wells and lingas distribute this promise through urban space. The city itself becomes a ritual body in which movement, residence and death acquire soteriological meaning.'
      ]},
      {title:'Regional myth and historical place',paragraphs:[
        'A later khanda may preserve stories of a temple whose historical phases are independently documented. The difference between literary and archaeological chronology is productive: it can reveal how a community retrospectively imagined its sacred centre.',
        'Purana regional history should therefore be written by triangulating text, inscription, architecture and local tradition, not by choosing one source as literal fact.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'The early Skandapurana is strongly Shaiva and particularly important for a period in which Shiva devotion, ascetic institutions and sacred geography were being systematically connected.',
      'Later khandas make sacred place itself a theological category. Shiva is not only transcendent Lord or linga but a presence distributed across cities, mountains, rivers and countless local forms.',
      'Skanda, Goddess, Ganesha and Vishnu traditions are incorporated into this world without erasing their identities. The divine family and broader pantheon make Shaiva supremacy socially and narratively comprehensive.',
      'Liberation can be mediated by geography. Kashi especially makes death in a particular city a soteriological event, while other mahatmyas promise merit, purification or divine encounter through pilgrimage.',
      'The plurality of later regional theologies prevents one systematic doctrine from representing the entire Skanda corpus. A Kashi chapter and a Prabhasa chapter may share Shaiva authority while articulating different local sacred priorities.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Skanda title became perhaps the most important Sanskrit container for regional pilgrimage scripture. Its khandas helped integrate Kashi, Ujjain, Prabhasa, Narmada and many other sacred landscapes into a shared Shaiva-Purana universe.',
      'Kashi Khanda had enormous independent reception. Pilgrims, priests and later writers could use it as a city scripture without reading the other khandas.',
      'The early Nepalese manuscripts transformed modern scholarship. Once critically edited, they demonstrated that the oldest recoverable Skandapurana is not identical with the huge printed corpus. This discovery changed broader theories of how Puranas grow.',
      'The text became a laboratory for studying canon by accretion. A canonical title can remain stable while the actual contents become regionally plural and massively larger.',
      'Modern pilgrimage traditions continue to interact with Skanda mahatmyas, but influence can move both ways. A practice may predate its extant textual description, while later print can standardize routes and myths.',
      'The Reva attribution controversy shows how twentieth-century print decisions can reshape the apparent canon. Bibliographic history is therefore part of religious history.',
      'For Shaivism, the early text offers something the later mahatmyas cannot: evidence closer to formative institutional worlds. For regional religion, the later corpus offers something the early text cannot: detailed maps of mature sacred landscapes. Both are indispensable but they answer different questions.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'Pilgrimage dominates the later corpus. Bathing, walking routes, sight of lingas, gifts, fasting and residence transform physical geography into repeated religious action.',
      'Kashi’s death practices reveal the intersection of urban institutions, funeral culture and liberation theology. Literary claims should be compared with historical patterns of migration, hospices and priestly service.',
      'Regional mahatmyas imply pilgrimage economies of temples, donors, guides and Brahmins. The texts prescribe merit and hierarchy but do not supply transparent economic statistics.',
      'Early Shaiva ascetic material offers evidence for religious specialists whose practices differ from the household pilgrimage world of later khandas. The title therefore spans distinct social formations.',
      'Women, kings, merchants, ascetics and ordinary pilgrims appear differently across local corpora. Social history must be written khanda by khanda rather than generalized from the entire “Skanda Purana.”'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The Groningen Skandapurana project is one of the most important modern Purana critical-edition enterprises. It reconstructs the early text from old Nepalese manuscripts and publishes Sanskrit with extensive philological analysis.',
      'That critical text should not be confused with a critical edition of the later seven-khanda vulgate. The later regional corpora require separate manuscript histories and editions.',
      'The first question in any citation is therefore which Skanda: early Skandapurana or named later khanda? A bare chapter number is often useless across recensions.',
      'Mahatmya attribution should be checked through manuscript colophons and catalogues. Reva proves that print can assign a text to the wrong Purana family for generations.',
      'Critical editing here is necessarily plural. The historical goal is not one “original complete Skanda Purana” but a genealogy of related textual bodies sharing a canonical name.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'The volumes and articles of Adriaensen, Bakker, Isaacson and the Groningen project are the required starting point for the early Skandapurana. Rocher remains useful for the later printed corpus.',
      'Regional khanda study should then move to specialist scholarship on Kashi, Ujjain, Prabhasa or Narmada, with inscriptions and archaeology used alongside the Sanskrit mahatmyas.'
    ]}
  ],[
    {title:'R. Adriaensen, H. T. Bakker and H. Isaacson — Towards a Critical Edition of the Skandapurana',detail:'Programmatic study of the early Nepalese manuscript tradition.',url:'https://research.rug.nl/en/publications/towards-a-critical-edition-of-the-skandapurana/'},
    {title:'Adriaensen, Bakker and Isaacson — The Skandapurana, Vol. I, Adhyayas 1–25',detail:'Critical Sanskrit text and prolegomena.',url:'https://research.rug.nl/en/publications/the-skandapurana-volume-1-adhyayas-1-25-critically-edited-with-pr/'},
    {title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Survey of the later Skanda corpus.'}
  ]);
})();